Hello--

This was my first and only, randomly chosen character in the
/dev/null tournament (sahara server).  Unfortunately, I didn't
actually ascend until nine or ten hours after the tournament had
ended, so rather than being able to claim the vaguely impressive
record of "1 for 1," my official result turned out to be 0/1 or
0/0.  That's fine with me, because I never would have ascended at
all if not for the time limit on the tournament.  I've been
playing sporadically for five or six years, but I've always had
trouble making the transition from a cautious, thorough, early-
game playing style to something more appropriate for Gehennom;
whenever I've reached the Valley, my games have bogged down in
item management, and I've gotten bored and walked away.  This
time, with the tournament deadline hanging over me, I finally
managed to adjust my priorities from "optimal advancement" to
"just ascend, @#$% it!"  Many thanks to Robin and the other
admins for running the event.

I had the great fortune early in the game of finding a shop with
a shield of reflection (it was surprisingly cheap!).  After that,
things went pretty smoothly.  In the past, I've always had to
stop several times over the course of a game, in tight battle
situations, to make an exhaustive list of my options--ending in
prayer and Elbereth.  This time, I don't recall that there ever
/were/ any such tight spots, and I certainly never needed to use
prayer or Elbereth to save myself.  (I accepted the challenge at
first, but when I found out that KoL was a whole separate game, I
decided to ignore it and focus on Nethack.  In the end, though, I
only used Elbereth by force of habit, when identifying wands.)  I
suppose this may be due in large part to my character being a
Valkyrie.

The scariest moment was in Minetown, when an arch-lich suddenly
appeared beside me (at just XL 11), and attempted to cast touch
of death (which failed, luckily).  I used my wand of digging to
make a quick exit and catch my breath.  After examining all the
items in the rest of the mines below, and using a polymorph trap
to turn my cats into dragons, I finally dared to climb back up to
town.  The arch-lich promptly teleported across the level to
greet me, and we engaged in battle . . . and then, after a
moment, it disappeared.  I used telepathy to look for it, both in
town and on the next few levels up, but I never saw it again.
Presumably, it was not an arch-lich at all (there were none in my
"vanquished" list), but a shape-shifter.

After that experience, I kept a ring of protection against shape
changers in my main inventory for almost all the rest of the
game.  Previously, I had thought that ring was mainly useful as a
defence against were-creatures--and therefore, that it was mostly
useless.  Now that I know how dangerous the shape-shifters can
be, I have a much greater appreciation for it.  (Later, I had a
lot of fun with a pet chameleon, until I thoughtlessly brought it
with me to Medusa's level, where it drowned itself.)

Other than that, there were few exciting events.  I didn't get MR
until relatively late, so I had some unfortunate encounters with
real liches, who destroyed my armor.  I also did some dumb things
(of course), like losing Mj=F6llnir to a BoH explosion (tricksy),
and eating a wolf when I was already satiated (somehow, luckily,
I didn't choke).  I also let mind flayers suck my brains a couple
of times, which was annoying.  Later, in Gehennom, I absent-
mindedly forgot to put my cloak of MR back on, and wandered into
a polymorph trap--losing a +5 shirt and the poly-self conduct
(not that I cared much about conducts).

The ascent run itself was pretty boring, even though it was all
new for me.  I was a little surprised and amused when Moloch hit
his own high-priest(ess?) with a lightning bolt in the Sanctum.
After all the time I'd spent digging and lighting paths between
stairs, it turned out that teleport control (courtesy ~20
peaceful tengu) plus a few wands of teleport were all I needed to
move quickly.  Generally, I had good luck with geography:  there
were plenty of early altars, I bumped into Vlad's stairs and the
vibrating square without searching, and I found the right Astral
altar on the first try.  (On the other hand, Minetown had only
two shops, and 6 candles.)  I allowed the Wizard an extra turn or
two whenever he showed up, because I was rushing, but even so he
was easily dispatched with wands of death.

In the future, I think I'd like to try playing without the help
of spoilers.  I'm already thoroughly spoiled, of course, so what
I mean is that I wouldn't consult spoilers during the course of
the game.  I think this should speed things up and make
everything more exciting.

